<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
  <title>Reading Quiz 9: Potential Energy, Energy Diagrams, and Harmonic Motion</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>Contents:</alert> This quiz covers sections 5.6, 5.7, and 6.1 through 6.3.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Instructions:</alert> Answer all the questions to the best of your ability on paper. When you are done, screenshot your work and upload 
      it as a pdf using the <q>File Upload</q> question below. When you submit the quiz, <alert>don't navigate away from Canvas.</alert> Scroll
      down to the bottom of the page for a download link to the solutions to the quiz.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	From the figure at the top of page 186, how can we tell that the particle will be accelerated towards the
            origin and reverse its motion whenever it reaches <m>x_1</m> and <m>x_2</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          		For the example of the pendulum in page 216, use the Taylor series for cosine, which is 
              <m>\cos\theta = 1-\frac{\theta^2}{2}+\frac{\theta^4}{4!}-\frac{\theta^6}{6!}+\ldots</m> 
                to find the approximate expression for U in the text
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          		Derive the expression for the kinetic energy of the pendulum, <m>K=\frac{1}{2}ml^2\dot{\theta}^2</m>.
              Explain your assumptions clearly and list any expressions from kinematics you may be using.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	The figure shows the potential energy function <m>V(r)</m> for a particular interaction, where <m>r</m> 
            is the distance between the particles interacting. Explain the behavior of the force 
            (attractive or repulsive) as a function of r
        </p>

          <image source="images/quiz-images/quiz9/energy_diagram.svg" width="60%">
                <shortdescription>Potential energy as a function of distance. The function
                  U of r is decreasing.
                </shortdescription>
                <description>
                  <p>
                    A diagram of potential energy as a function of distance. The potential energy
                    is monotonically decreasing with distance <m>x</m>.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    At low values of <m>x</m>, the potential energy is very high and positive. It
                    drops abruptly, crossing the positive axis some distance away from the origin.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    At higher values of <m>x</m>, the potential energy continues decreasing but not
                    as quickly, with the slope of the graph levelling off.
                  </p>
                </description>
          </image>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          In the hypothetical atomic interaction shown in the figure, assume that the atoms initially have zero 
          velocity at a separation of 0.2nm. How will their separation evolve in time? What happens if initially
          their separation is 0.4nm?
        </p>

          <image source="images/quiz-images/quiz9/energy_diagram_2.svg" width="100%">
            <shortdescription>A potential energy diagram for two atoms.</shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>
                A potential energy diagram for the interaction of two atoms. The x axis is in units
                of nm, going from 0 to 0.8. The y axis is in units of <m>10^{-21}</m> joules, going from
                -12 to 15.
              </p>
              <p>
                At low values of <m>x</m>, the potential energy is positive and very high, dropping abruptly.
                The potential energy is 0 at <m>x=0.1 nm</m>. It reaches a minimum of roughly -11 at roughly
                <m>x=0.12 nm</m>. 
              </p>
              <p>
                The potential energy grows between <m>x=0.12 nm</m> and <m>x=0.35 nm</m>, reaching 0 at
                <m>x=0.2 nm</m> and a positive maximum of roughly 5.
              </p>
              <p>
                For <m>x &gt; 0.35nm</m>, the potential energy is positive and decreasing, seeming to reach
                a horizontal asymptote at 0 energy.
              </p>
            </description>    
          </image>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

  </page>
</worksheet>
